1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,433
A deploy script is supposed to record the
commit ID it deployed, the fingerprint of

2
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:14,227
one exact snapshot. One morning the record
holds the text "no-such-branch" where

3
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:17,763
forty hexadecimal digits should
be. The script didn't fail.

4
00:00:17,803 --> 00:00:22,556
It didn't warn. It carried a branch name
that doesn't exist all the way into the

5
00:00:22,596 --> 00:00:23,395
deploy record.

6
00:00:23,735 --> 00:00:28,154
Your CTO asks: how can a tool ask Git
for a commit and get back a word?

7
00:00:28,194 --> 00:00:33,367
The answer is in how git rev-parse behaves
when it can't resolve a name, and it's a

8
00:00:33,407 --> 00:00:36,507
one-flag fix. Hold on to
that word. It comes back.

9
00:00:38,347 --> 00:00:42,426
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:42,466 --> 00:00:47,828
Every Git command that takes a revision,
git log main, git show HEAD~2, git diff

11
00:00:47,868 --> 00:00:51,339
v1.0.0, turns that argument
into an object ID first.

12
00:00:51,379 --> 00:00:56,877
A revision is any expression that names an
object. The resolver behind all of them

13
00:00:56,917 --> 00:01:02,211
is one command, git rev-parse. Today you
use it directly, for two jobs: turning

14
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:06,465
names into IDs, and asking a
repository questions about itself.

15
00:01:06,805 --> 00:01:10,728
git rev-parse only reads. It
is 🟢 SAFE: it changes nothing.

16
00:01:11,068 --> 00:01:17,526
We replay labs/ch03/rev-parse.sh, on the
same inference-service repository as the

17
00:01:17,566 --> 00:01:23,864
previous video, so you'll recognize the
IDs. Here it is: four commits, with the

18
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:27,394
branch main and the tag
v1.0.0 on the merge.

19
00:01:29,234 --> 00:01:30,646
After this video you can:

20
00:01:30,986 --> 00:01:36,023
Resolve any revision expression to an object
ID and verify that it names a commit.

21
00:01:36,363 --> 00:01:41,006
Ask a repository for its directory, its
top level and its formats from a script.

22
00:01:41,346 --> 00:01:44,935
Explain the order in which Git
resolves an ambiguous short name.

23
00:01:45,275 --> 00:01:48,674
Say why a tool should store
full IDs and not abbreviations.

24
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:55,469
Why does this command matter? Because scripts
that guess are the scripts that break.

25
00:01:55,509 --> 00:02:00,405
A script that assumes where .git is, or
that a name is a branch, or that a captured

26
00:02:00,445 --> 00:02:04,033
string is an ID, works until the
day the assumption is false.

27
00:02:04,073 --> 00:02:07,422
git rev-parse replaces each
guess with a question to Git.

28
00:02:07,762 --> 00:02:12,472
In one sentence: git rev-parse resolves
anything that can name an object to its

29
00:02:12,512 --> 00:02:17,462
object ID and answers questions about the
repository itself, by the same rules that

30
00:02:17,502 --> 00:02:20,408
every other Git command
applies to its arguments.

31
00:02:20,748 --> 00:02:25,149
The revision syntax, with the
values from the demo repository.

32
00:02:25,189 --> 00:02:31,165
A ref is a name that holds an object ID,
and a ref name such as main, v1.0.0 or HEAD

33
00:02:31,205 --> 00:02:36,609
means the object the ref names. For an
annotated tag, that's the tag object.

34
00:02:36,649 --> 00:02:40,763
HEAD~2 means two steps back,
following first parents only.

35
00:02:41,103 --> 00:02:48,343
HEAD^2 means the second parent of a merge.
HEAD^1, or HEAD^ alone, is the first.

36
00:02:48,383 --> 00:02:55,805
HEAD^{tree} means the object of that type
reached by following the name: here, the

37
00:02:55,845 --> 00:03:03,358
commit's tree. v1.0.0^{commit} peels the
tag to a commit, and v1.0.0^{} peels until

38
00:03:03,398 --> 00:03:05,269
it's no longer a tag.

39
00:03:05,609 --> 00:03:10,695
HEAD:src/server.py is the blob or
tree at that path in that commit.

40
00:03:10,735 --> 00:03:17,122
A leading colon, as in :config.toml, reads
the entry for that path in the index, the

41
00:03:17,162 --> 00:03:21,713
staging area, and :2:config.toml
reads the entry at stage 2.

42
00:03:21,753 --> 00:03:28,140
And main@{1} is the previous value of the
ref, read from its reflog, the ref's local

43
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:28,752
journal.

44
00:03:29,092 --> 00:03:34,810
Now the internals of name lookup. A bare
name is looked up in a fixed order: first

45
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:40,498
$GIT_DIR/<name>, then refs/<name>, then
refs/tags/<name>, then refs/heads/<name>,

46
00:03:40,538 --> 00:03:44,711
then refs/remotes/<name>, and
last refs/remotes/<name>/HEAD.

47
00:03:44,751 --> 00:03:50,399
Tags come before branches in that list. A
tag therefore wins over a branch of the

48
00:03:50,439 --> 00:03:54,893
same name. Git 2.55 prints warning:
refname 'main' is ambiguous.

49
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:59,598
and uses the tag. In scripts, write
the full name, refs/heads/main.

50
00:03:59,938 --> 00:04:06,682
The second family of options describes the
repository: --git-dir, --show-toplevel,

51
00:04:06,722 --> 00:04:12,639
--show-prefix, --show-cdup,
--is-inside-work-tree, --is-bare-repository,

52
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:17,190
--show-object-format,
--show-ref-format and --git-path.

53
00:04:17,230 --> 00:04:22,568
These are what a script uses in
place of assumptions about paths.

54
00:04:22,908 --> 00:04:27,555
When should you not rely on it without
care? Without --verify, the command is a

55
00:04:27,595 --> 00:04:32,538
sorter of arguments, not a validator. That's
the failure mode of the hook, and we'll

56
00:04:32,578 --> 00:04:33,547
see it on screen.

57
00:04:35,387 --> 00:04:39,857
Think of git rev-parse as the front desk
that every other Git command passes

58
00:04:39,897 --> 00:04:44,545
through. You hand over a name in any of
the forms Git understands, and the desk

59
00:04:44,585 --> 00:04:48,046
hands back the one thing that
is unambiguous: an object ID.

60
00:04:48,386 --> 00:04:53,213
The model breaks in one place. A front
desk refuses a visitor it can't identify.

61
00:04:53,253 --> 00:04:58,019
git rev-parse without --verify doesn't
refuse: its first purpose is to sort the

62
00:04:58,059 --> 00:05:02,947
arguments of a calling script into revisions
and everything else, so what isn't a

63
00:05:02,987 --> 00:05:07,144
revision is passed through. You have
to ask for the strict behaviour.

64
00:05:07,484 --> 00:05:12,550
One more rule belongs to the model, and
it comes from Chapter 2: names can be

65
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:18,053
ambiguous, full object IDs cannot. An
abbreviation is unique only in one repository

66
00:05:18,093 --> 00:05:23,491
at one time. An abbreviated ID in a script
or a ticket can become ambiguous as the

67
00:05:23,531 --> 00:05:25,679
repository grows. Store full IDs.

68
00:05:27,519 --> 00:05:34,265
The root commit b602c1f is on the left. Two
commits follow it: 4f2cc0c on the lower

69
00:05:34,305 --> 00:05:39,498
line and 62001eb on the upper
line, the branch feature/batching.

70
00:05:39,538 --> 00:05:45,385
They meet in the merge 0c2cf43, where
main, the tag v1.0.0 and HEAD are.

71
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:51,895
Now the labels. HEAD^1 is the first parent,
the lower line, and it's the same commit

72
00:05:51,935 --> 00:05:55,960
as HEAD~1. HEAD^2 is the
second parent, the upper line.

73
00:05:56,300 --> 00:06:01,980
HEAD~2 is two generations back along first
parents, which is HEAD^1^1, the root.

74
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:07,127
Say the rule out loud: tilde counts
generations, caret chooses a parent.

75
00:06:15,967 --> 00:06:21,670
Predict, then look. I'm about to ask for
HEAD~2, HEAD^2 and HEAD^1, in that order.

76
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:25,873
Point at each commit on the
graph and say its ID. I'll wait.

77
00:06:30,713 --> 00:06:35,775
Quick quiz: v1.0.0, then
v1.0.0 peeled to a commit.

78
00:06:35,815 --> 00:06:40,578
One ID twice, or two
different IDs? Your answer?

79
00:06:48,418 --> 00:06:53,704
HEAD~2 is the root, HEAD^2 is the commit of
the feature branch, HEAD^1 is the commit

80
00:06:53,744 --> 00:06:58,966
on the first-parent line. The last command
prints two different IDs: the tag object

81
00:06:59,006 --> 00:07:04,228
first, then the commit it peels to, which
is HEAD. If you said one ID twice, you're

82
00:07:04,268 --> 00:07:05,243
in good company.

83
00:07:05,583 --> 00:07:09,831
The other direction: from HEAD or
a short name to a full ref name.

84
00:07:10,171 --> 00:07:15,743
--abbrev-ref prints the short branch name.
--symbolic-full-name prints the full ref,

85
00:07:15,783 --> 00:07:19,485
and shows you which namespace
a short name was found in.

86
00:07:19,525 --> 00:07:24,563
main@{1} comes from the reflog. The last
line reads the index, not a commit.

87
00:07:24,903 --> 00:07:29,994
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a
repository in the lab shell: git rev-parse

88
00:07:30,034 --> 00:07:33,688
--abbrev-ref HEAD, then git
rev-parse HEAD. I'll wait.

89
00:07:38,528 --> 00:07:41,576
A short branch name first,
then a full commit ID.

90
00:07:41,916 --> 00:07:43,597
Questions about the repository.

91
00:07:43,937 --> 00:07:49,692
At the top level, --git-dir prints .git.
What will it print from two directories

92
00:07:49,732 --> 00:07:51,285
down? Say it out loud.

93
00:07:56,125 --> 00:08:01,867
--git-dir answers relative to where you
are. --show-prefix and --show-cdup are the

94
00:08:01,907 --> 00:08:06,520
path from the top level down to
the current directory and back up.

95
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:12,301
--show-object-format and --show-ref-format
name the two storage formats that later

96
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:17,730
videos of this part cover. --git-path
gives the location of a file inside the

97
00:08:17,770 --> 00:08:22,947
repository, and stays right when the
repository is a linked worktree or an

98
00:08:22,987 --> 00:08:25,908
environment variable relocates part of it.

99
00:08:26,248 --> 00:08:29,428
Now the hook. Error handling
is where scripts go wrong.

100
00:08:29,768 --> 00:08:34,521
The first line asks for a branch that
doesn't exist and hides the error stream.

101
00:08:34,561 --> 00:08:39,132
What ends up in the variable: nothing,
an error message, or the name itself?

102
00:08:39,172 --> 00:08:39,860
Your answer?

103
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:49,558
The variable holds the text
no-such-branch, and the status is 128.

104
00:08:49,598 --> 00:08:55,793
That's the word in the deploy record,
explained. With --verify --quiet, the variable

105
00:08:55,833 --> 00:09:01,804
is empty and the status is 1. --short
implies --verify, so it accepts exactly one

106
00:09:01,844 --> 00:09:06,183
revision. And a merge with two
parents has no third parent.

107
00:09:13,723 --> 00:09:19,513
One limit of --verify: it accepts exactly
one argument and prints one ID or nothing.

108
00:09:19,553 --> 00:09:25,273
It checks that the argument can be turned
into an ID, not that the object is in the

109
00:09:25,313 --> 00:09:30,061
database. A full forty-digit ID
passes even if no such object exists.

110
00:09:30,101 --> 00:09:35,405
The peeling suffix closes that gap,
because ^{commit} has to read the object.

111
00:09:37,245 --> 00:09:38,893
Five mistakes to watch for.

112
00:09:39,233 --> 00:09:42,646
Capturing the output of git
rev-parse without --verify.

113
00:09:42,686 --> 00:09:47,920
Root cause: an unresolvable argument is
printed on standard output and only the exit

114
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,616
status reports the failure.

115
00:09:49,956 --> 00:09:55,918
Trusting --verify to prove an object exists.
Root cause: it checks that the argument

116
00:09:55,958 --> 00:10:01,564
can be turned into an ID, and a full ID
passes without a lookup; add ^{commit}.

117
00:10:01,904 --> 00:10:05,974
Using a short name in a script
when a tag and a branch share it.

118
00:10:06,014 --> 00:10:11,175
Root cause: the lookup order puts refs/tags/
before refs/heads/, so the tag wins.

119
00:10:11,515 --> 00:10:17,154
Confusing ~2 with ^2. Root cause: tilde
counts generations along first parents,

120
00:10:17,194 --> 00:10:19,597
caret chooses a parent of a merge.

121
00:10:19,937 --> 00:10:24,930
Storing abbreviated IDs. Root cause:
an abbreviation is unique only in one

122
00:10:24,970 --> 00:10:26,494
repository at one time.

123
00:10:28,334 --> 00:10:33,368
Now, out of the lab. A backend team keeps
a release script in a subdirectory of the

124
00:10:33,408 --> 00:10:38,319
repository, and it's called from CI, from
laptops and from a cron host, each time

125
00:10:38,359 --> 00:10:43,270
from a different directory. The script
begins with git rev-parse --show-toplevel,

126
00:10:43,310 --> 00:10:46,448
which makes it independent
of the calling directory.

127
00:10:46,788 --> 00:10:53,193
To test whether the branch to release exists,
it runs git rev-parse --verify --quiet

128
00:10:53,233 --> 00:10:59,638
"refs/heads/$branch^{commit}", with nothing
to parse: the exit status is the answer.

129
00:10:59,678 --> 00:11:05,852
And when two people disagree about what a
build contained, they run git rev-parse

130
00:11:05,892 --> 00:11:10,148
HEAD^{tree} in both checkouts.
Equal tree IDs settle it.

131
00:11:11,988 --> 00:11:16,295
Your turn. Do Exercise 17.5,
"What git rev-parse answers", in

132
00:11:16,335 --> 00:11:22,139
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md. It's a
command-prediction exercise: write down the

133
00:11:22,179 --> 00:11:27,911
output of every git rev-parse call before
you run it, and for IDs say whether two

134
00:11:27,951 --> 00:11:33,826
outputs are equal or different. Pay attention
to what changes when HEAD is detached

135
00:11:33,866 --> 00:11:35,679
and when a file is staged.

136
00:11:36,019 --> 00:11:41,677
The challenge is Exercise 16.8, "Scripts
that read .git by hand", in the same file.

137
00:11:43,517 --> 00:11:46,218
Question 54 of the CTO question bank:

138
00:11:46,558 --> 00:11:51,243
A strong answer says what an abbreviation
is unique within, and over what time.

139
00:11:51,283 --> 00:11:56,268
It describes how the failure shows up later,
as the repository grows, and names what

140
00:11:56,308 --> 00:12:00,993
the tool should store and how a script
obtains that value safely, including the

141
00:12:01,033 --> 00:12:05,599
check that the value names a commit. The
model answer is in the answers file.

142
00:12:05,639 --> 00:12:07,214
Give yours first, out loud.

143
00:12:13,554 --> 00:12:17,435
Let's land this. Names go
in, one object ID comes out.

144
00:12:17,775 --> 00:12:19,435
You should now be able to say:

145
00:12:19,775 --> 00:12:23,690
git rev-parse is the resolver behind
every command that takes a revision.

146
00:12:24,030 --> 00:12:30,131
Tilde counts generations along first parents;
caret chooses a parent; ^{type} peels

147
00:12:30,171 --> 00:12:34,496
to an object of that type; a
leading colon reads the index.

148
00:12:34,836 --> 00:12:40,037
A short name is looked up in a fixed order,
tags before branches, so scripts write

149
00:12:40,077 --> 00:12:40,995
full ref names.

150
00:12:41,335 --> 00:12:49,474
In scripts, --verify --quiet with ^{commit}
gives one ID or a failing exit status.

151
00:12:49,814 --> 00:12:54,507
Ask Git for the repository's directory,
top level and formats; store full IDs.

152
00:12:56,347 --> 00:12:58,918
Read section 3.6 of Chapter 3.

153
00:12:59,258 --> 00:13:03,398
Today you turned names into IDs,
and repaired a script that guessed.

154
00:13:03,438 --> 00:13:08,500
Practise that before the next video:
packfiles, pack indexes and delta compression.

155
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:11,697
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

156
00:13:11,737 --> 00:13:13,172
See you in the next one.
